    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-105664",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c",
      "knowledge_point": "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108829/assets/5643e87bfb5dfb5c29750f48abffe2443bf656901d74a47a0aa39bddfd0c1590.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has a curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108829/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I, II, and IV only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II,  III, and IV only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "II and III only",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-105664",
        "kp_title": "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-105092",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c",
      "knowledge_point": "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108829/assets/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108829/assets/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I, III, and IV only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "I and III only",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_revealed_solution"
        }
      ],
      "source_answer_policy": {
